SONAR SHOCK — COMMUNITY BUGFIX PATCH (for upstream)
====================================================
From a player, with respect — the game is great; these are fixes for the
bugs that hurt the most in long sessions (getting permanently stuck after
crouching, the framerate collapsing over time, getting stuck holding a
barrel that the game refuses to put down, and stuttering — worst on
floor 3), plus a few small ones found along the way.

Scope: 25 GDScript files, 270 insertions / 80 deletions. No scenes, resources,
project settings, or save formats are touched. All added helpers/vars are
prefixed sp_ to avoid collisions. The diff was taken against the scripts of
the 4.2.2 Steam build (they ship as plain text in the pck), so it should apply
cleanly to the project unless these files changed after that build.

HOW TO APPLY
------------
Paths are relative to the Godot project root (the folder with project.godot):

    git apply SonarShock_bugfixes.patch        # in a git checkout
    patch -p1 < SonarShock_bugfixes.patch      # plain patch(1)

Everything below explains each change; the unified diff follows at the end.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PLAYER PERMANENTLY STUCK UNDER OBJECTS AFTER CROUCHING   (player.gd)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause — two problems combining:

  a) Standing up is gated by $crouch_check, a single RayCast3D on the
     player's center axis (origin y=0.95, pointing up 1.2 m). The player's
     body is a capsule of radius 0.5. Crouched under the EDGE of an overhang
     (a shelf/table/duct that covers part of the capsule but not the center
     axis), the ray reports "clear" while the standing capsule would
     intersect the obstacle.

  b) The crouch collider change is done by the "crouch" AnimationPlayer's
     "new_animation" animating body:scale from (1,1,1) to (1,0.5,1).
     Non-uniform scaling of collision shapes is unsupported by Godot physics
     (capsules especially — the editor warns about this), and on standing up
     the capsule re-grows interpolated over 0.2 s AFTER the ray check passed,
     so the player can also drift further under an obstacle while the
     collider is still growing.

  Consequence: the standing capsule ends up deeply overlapping static
  geometry. move_and_slide() cannot recover from deep penetration, so the
  player is wedged permanently — walking and standing both fail, and only
  reloading helps. Respawning also force-uncrouched with no check at all
  (second occurrence in _process), which could wedge a player who saved
  while crouched somewhere low.

What the patch does (player.gd only, scene untouched):

  * sp_apply_crouch_shape(is_crouched): resizes the capsule in code,
    instantly — height 2.0 <-> 1.0 and CollisionShape3D position y
    1.0 <-> 0.5118. These are numerically identical to the END pose of the
    original animation, so every existing crawl space still works exactly as
    designed. Scale stays (1,1,1). The capsule resource is duplicate()d once
    in _ready so the height edit cannot leak through a shared resource.

  * sp_patch_crouch_animation() (called first thing in _ready): removes the
    two tracks targeting "body" (scale + position) from "new_animation" at
    runtime; the head/camera dip and crouchvis tracks are kept, so the crouch
    FEEL is unchanged.
      -> Editor-side alternative: simply delete those two tracks from the
         animation in the editor and keep the rest of this patch; the
         function then finds nothing to remove and becomes a no-op. That is
         the cleaner permanent fix — the runtime surgery exists only because
         this patch avoids touching scene files.

  * sp_can_stand(): replaces the ray as the stand-up gate with a
    direct-space intersect_shape query of a standing-sized capsule
    (radius 0.48, height 1.9, positioned so its top is flush at y=2.0 —
    slightly shrunk/lifted so the floor underfoot and grazing walls cannot
    false-positive), using the player's own collision_mask and excluding the
    player's RID. If the space state is unavailable it falls back to the old
    ray. The crouch_check node itself is left in the scene.

  * The collider now changes in the SAME frame as a passed check (and the
    crouch input block runs after move_and_slide), which closes the 0.2 s
    grow-after-check race from (b).

  * Anti-embed watchdog (in _physics_process, every 10 physics frames): if a
    STANDING player is penetrating geometry from above — detected via
    PhysicsServer3D.body_test_motion with zero motion, a contact normal
    pointing down (normal.y < -0.6) and depth > 0.08 — the player is
    automatically crouched ("Forced to crouch.") and thereby freed. This
    rescues every remaining path into the wedged state (doors/elevators
    closing onto the player, and saves made while already stuck by the old
    bug). It is gated on !crouch/!dead/!floating/!player_inactive and showed
    zero false triggers in a 300-frame movement soak test.

  * The respawn force-uncrouch is now gated on sp_can_stand(); if there is
    no headroom the player simply stays crouched.

  Compatibility notes: enemies aim using new_manager.cur_crouch — that
  mirror is untouched. Gamemanager.crouched save semantics are unchanged.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. FRAMERATE COLLAPSE OVER LONG SESSIONS ("memory leak")
   (new_manager.gd + eight decal scripts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause:

  * Bullet holes, casings, and hit-blood are spawned with
    get_tree().get_root().add_child(...) (~200 sites in player.gd), so they
    live ABOVE the current scene and survive level transitions, death, and
    quitting to the menu — change_scene_to_file() only swaps current_scene.

  * The decal-lifetime option's "OFF" value (decal_lifetime >= 90, the
    default) disables every timed self-free branch (casing1.gd even stops
    its own deletion Timer), making every decal immortal.

  * deletedecals() cleans groups "bullethole" and "delete_upon_transition",
    but blood_sprite.tscn (the per-hit blood, spawned once per pellet/hit)
    is only in group "blood_splatter" — it was never cleaned ANYWHERE and
    accumulated for the entire process lifetime.

  After a few hours, thousands of live Node3Ds (AnimatedSprite3D +
  AudioStreamPlayer3D each) accumulate and the frame rate collapses until a
  full restart — consistent with the "Memory Leak?" reports on the Steam
  forum.

What the patch does:

  * new_manager gains a small bounded FIFO registry:
    register_decal(node, category) with caps SP_DECAL_CAPS =
    {bullethole: 300, blood: 250, casing: 150} (constants — tune freely).
    When a category exceeds its cap, the oldest entry is queue_free()d, and
    invalid references are pruned as a side effect. The newest few hundred
    decals — everything a player can actually see — remain.

  * Each decal scene registers itself with ONE added line at the top of its
    _ready. Registration lives in the decal scripts, so none of the ~200
    spawn sites needed changing and enemy-spawned decals are covered too.
    Registered: bullethole2.gd (also used by bullethole3.tscn),
    bullethole5.gd (also used by bullethole6.tscn), bullethole7.gd,
    bullethole_melee.gd, sword_bullethole.gd, blood_splatter.gd,
    blood_sprite.gd, random/casing1.gd.
    (bullethole4.gd and explosive_bullethole.gd already free themselves on
    their own timers and were deliberately left out.)

  * deletedecals() additionally frees group "blood_splatter" — this is the
    actual fix for the never-cleaned hit-blood — and sweeps the registry.

  * The options menu is untouched: "OFF" still means "no timed fade"; it is
    now bounded by the caps instead of leaking without limit. Values below
    90 behave exactly as before.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. LOW-SANITY ELEVATOR RIDE CAN SOFT-LOCK   (Maps/main_scene.gd)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  _crazy_elevator_ride() rolls the destination with
  randi_range(0.6, 5.4). randi_range takes ints, so GDScript truncates this
  to randi_range(0, 5) — and a result of 0 matches none of the == 1..5
  branches. Everything, including freeing the old map, spawning the new one,
  and REOPENING THE DOORS, lives inside those branches, so on a 0 the
  elevator closes and nothing else happens: a ~1-in-6 soft-lock whenever the
  crazy ride triggers (mental health <= 40). Changed to randi_range(1, 5).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. CARRIED BARREL / MINE CAN NEVER BE PUT DOWN — "Not enough space."
   (player.gd)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause — the drop check denies far too much, and two items have no
throw escape:

  * Dropping a carried item is gated by spawner/Area3D under the camera —
    a fixed 0.65 m cube ~1.25 m ahead at mid-height. ANY overlapping body
    denies with "Not enough space.": no filtering, and no relation to the
    carried item's actual size.

  * Crouching lowers the head by 0.5, which sinks the bottom of that cube
    BELOW the floor plane — while crouched, the floor itself denies every
    placement, without exception.

  * The volume cannot be aimed (items always spawn at the fixed point), so
    any wall, step, or neighbouring prop inside the cube also denies — e.g.
    barrels on Map3 stand 1.07 m apart, closer than the cube is wide.

  * Crates, fire extinguishers and flares can always be THROWN as an
    escape, but the barrel and soma-mine throws repeat the same overlap
    check. A blocked volume therefore means the barrel can never leave the
    player's hands — and while carrying, every weapon is disabled: a
    soft-lock until the player happens onto open ground.

  * (The item_spawn_check forward ray was meant as a second gate but sits
    INSIDE the overlap loop, so it only runs when the area already denied —
    dead code.)

What the patch does (player.gd only, scene untouched):

  * sp_can_place(check_volume): the three copies of the check collapse into
    one helper. It promotes the item_spawn_check ray to the (now actually
    reachable) point-blank-wall gate, and replaces the Area3D test with a
    direct-space intersect_shape query of a cylinder sized to the carried
    item (barrel r 0.40 h 1.0, crate r 0.35 h 0.6, small items r 0.25
    h 0.4), anchored just ABOVE the spawn point to match how the props
    actually occupy space — so flat floors can no longer false-positive,
    crouched or not. The player's own RID is excluded.

  * Drops (interact) check ray + volume. The barrel and soma-mine throws
    now check only the ray, exactly like every other item's throw always
    did — turning toward any open direction works, so the carry state can
    no longer soft-lock, while the ray still prevents throwing an item
    into or beyond a point-blank wall.

  * The denial sound no longer restarts every physics frame while the
    throw button is held.

  Compatibility notes: the Area3D and item_spawn_check nodes stay in the
  scene (the area is simply no longer consulted; the helper falls back to
  it if the physics space is unavailable). A genuine point-blank wall
  still denies, so the game feel is unchanged.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. STUTTERING / HITCHES, WORST ON FLOOR 3
   (Maps/Map3.gd, Maps/main_scene.gd, blood_sprite.gd, steam_jumpscare.gd,
    11 enemy scripts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Several independent causes; each fix is small and self-contained.

  a) The Map3 security-monitor "video".  The screen on
     objects/Camera2 plays a SpriteFrames animation of 1,314 separate
     192x108 textures (~32 MB) at 45 fps, loop + autoplay, with no
     visibility culling — it swaps to a different GPU texture every ~22 ms
     from level entry, forever, even through walls. (For scale: the next
     largest animation in the game is 31 frames.)  Map3.gd _ready now
     attaches a VisibleOnScreenNotifier3D to the sprite and pauses it
     whenever it is off screen; it plays at full rate only while actually
     being looked at. Null-guarded, so the map loads fine even if the node
     is renamed later.

  b) Enemy look_at() error spam.  While pathing, enemies aim a helper node
     with look_at(next_loc, Vector3.UP) every physics frame. next_loc comes
     from NavigationAgent3D.get_next_path_position(), which returns the
     agent's OWN position once the path is finished — i.e. exactly when the
     enemy reaches the player. The direction then degenerates (equal or
     vertically-aligned with UP) and Godot prints an engine error EVERY
     PHYSICS FRAME PER ADJACENT ENEMY. Synchronous console/stderr writes on
     the main thread are a classic Windows stutter source, and this fires
     precisely during melee combat. All 13 such call sites (11 enemy
     scripts) now skip the call when the target's horizontal offset from
     the aiming node is negligible — which is also what the failed call
     did, minus the error.

  c) Navigation fan-out.  main_scene.gd pushed the player position to every
     living enemy via call_group() EVERY physics tick. The enemies only
     sample their path on their own timers (that is what the "path quality"
     option scales), but re-setting the target 60x/s dirties every
     NavigationAgent3D so each sampled query is a full cold repath. The
     fan-out now runs every 0.15 s; a target at most 0.15 s stale is
     imperceptible and combat behavior is unchanged.

  d) Blood decal aiming.  blood_sprite.gd aims splatter decals with
     look_at(point - normal, Vector3.UP) and only RETRIES with
     Vector3.RIGHT if the surface normal is exactly (0,+/-1,0) — so every
     floor/ceiling splat first fires a failed look_at and its engine error
     (one stderr write per splat, i.e. per pellet hit in combat). The up
     vector is now chosen BEFORE the call. This also fixes silently wrong
     decal orientation on near-but-not-exactly-horizontal surfaces, which
     the exact-equality check missed.

  e) Crypt threshold (floor 3).  Crossing the trigger_cyber area toggles
     LightmapGI and fog, which can stall on first-use shader variants at
     the doorway. Map3.gd now applies that rendering state for two frames
     during map load (while the elevator still hides the world) so the
     variants compile behind the loading pause instead. Best-effort: a
     no-op if nothing renders during those frames.

  f) steam_jumpscare.gd printed two debug lines mid-game on the main
     thread when fetching the Steam avatar for the low-sanity jumpscare;
     removed.

  Not addressed here (noted for completeness): the floor-3 entry load spike
  also caused by (a) — the 1,314 textures are hard dependencies of
  Map3.tscn and would need the scene itself re-authored (e.g. as a
  spritesheet); scenes are deliberately out of scope for this patch.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. SMALL FIXES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Maps/main_scene.gd: map teardown during elevator transitions used
    i.free() (immediate deletion) on the live map from inside an await
    chain. Replaced with queue_free() — the code then waits 2 s before
    instancing the next map, so behavior is identical, without the risk of
    freeing nodes that are still mid-callback that frame.

  * bullethole4.gd: "$AnimatedSprite3D.speed_scale == randf_range(0.8, 1.2)"
    used == (a comparison whose result is discarded) instead of =, so the
    dragonbreath flicker variance never applied. (bullethole2.gd already has
    the correct = version of this line.)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW THIS WAS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A headless Godot 4.2.2 test harness mounts the shipped pck, instantiates
  the real player.tscn over synthetic collision geometry — including the
  exact partial-edge-overhang setup from bug 1a that the old ray check
  misses — drives crouch/movement through the input system, and asserts 69
  checks: crouch/uncrouch toggling, denial under full and edge overhangs, no
  embedding, watchdog rescue of a deliberately embedded standing player
  within 25 physics frames, zero watchdog false-positives across a 300-frame
  movement soak, the decal caps (500 spawned bulletholes settle at 300), the
  deletedecals()/blood cleanup paths, item placement (standing AND
  fully-crouched barrel drops in the open, denial at a point-blank wall and
  over a blocked spawn volume, the barrel-throw escape, and crate/mine drops
  through the shared path), and the stutter fixes (a live enemy ticking with
  an equal and a vertically-aligned nav target without a single look_at
  engine error reaching stderr — the runner fails if any does — plus static
  asserts that all 13 guards, the monitor culling, and the fan-out throttle
  are present). All 93 pass with this patch; the edge-overhang, cap,
  crouched-placement, and look_at-spam tests fail on the unpatched scripts,
  as expected. The harness needs no changes to the project and is available
  on request.

